      "writtenOn": "2026-09-24T18:37:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In general, the reliability of bash scripts tends to easily miss things like this. Can you rewrite them in a simplistic and easy-to-read way?\n\nI\u0027d rewrite bash in Python, keeping a thin bash entry point only because the CI scripts and builder.sh are bash.\n\nThe issues in this patch mostly come from bash\u0027s error semantics, not from carelessness:\n\nset -e is silently turned off inside any function called with || true, which is how an ES error turned into a PASS.\n\nA failure inside $( … ) is lost unless it\u0027s the last command.\n\nAn empty string compares as 0 in awk, so a missing metric passes a [0, 0] gate.\n\nA single \u0026 quietly makes things concurrent.\n\nPython reverses the default: errors raise exceptions and stop the run unless you deliberately catch them. That\u0027s the property you want in a gate.\n\nIt also costs nothing new:\n\nThe script already requires python3 (it checks for it at the top).\n\nderive_bounds.py is already Python.\n\nCI already runs tox, so unit tests come for free.\n\nThe standard library covers everything (urllib, json, subprocess, dataclasses), so there are no pip dependencies to install in the CI image.\n\nDisciplined bash (shellcheck, bats tests, strict helpers) can work, but you have to fight the language on every line, and reviewers have to know its traps to catch mistakes.\n\nAdditional details and the suggested sketch: https://trello.com/c/bjyYFhKX#comment-6ab56990021ce871fd8cea51",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 63,
        "startChar": 2,
        "endLine": 68,
        "endChar": 24
      },
      "revId": "05f146f50770ec3bed8daf77497acec4705d1844",
      "serverId": "d5d70762-12d0-45a1-890d-524b12d3f735"
    },
